(go/type Cmd "Cmd represents an external command being prepared or run.\n\nA Cmd cannot be reused after calling its [Cmd.Start], [Cmd.Run],\n[Cmd.Output], or [Cmd.CombinedOutput] methods.\n" (struct





    ^{:line 154 :tag string :doc "Path is the path of the command to run.\n\nThis is the only field that must be set to a non-zero\nvalue. If Path is relative, it is evaluated relative\nto Dir.\n"} Path





    ^{:line 160 :tag (slice string) :doc "Args holds command line arguments, including the command as Args[0].\nIf the Args field is empty or nil, Run uses {Path}.\n\nIn typical use, both Path and Args are set by calling Command.\n"} Args











    ^{:line 172 :tag (slice string) :doc "Env specifies the environment of the process.\nEach entry is of the form \"key=value\".\nIf Env is nil, the new process uses the current process's\nenvironment.\nIf Env contains duplicate environment keys, only the last\nvalue in the slice for each duplicate key is used.\nAs a special case on Windows, SYSTEMROOT is always added if\nmissing and not explicitly set to the empty string.\n\nSee also the Dir field, which may set PWD in the environment.\n"} Env


















    ^{:line 191 :tag string :doc "Dir specifies the working directory of the command.\nIf Dir is the empty string, Run runs the command in the\ncalling process's current directory.\n\nOn Unix systems, the value of Dir also determines the\nchild process's PWD environment variable if not otherwise\nspecified. A Unix process represents its working directory\nnot by name but as an implicit reference to a node in the\nfile tree. So, if the child process obtains its working\ndirectory by calling a function such as C's getcwd, which\ncomputes the canonical name by walking up the file tree, it\nwill not recover the original value of Dir if that value\nwas an alias involving symbolic links. However, if the\nchild process calls Go's [os.Getwd] or GNU C's\nget_current_dir_name, and the value of PWD is an alias for\nthe current directory, those functions will return the\nvalue of PWD, which matches the value of Dir.\n"} Dir



















    ^{:line 211 :tag io/Reader :doc "Stdin specifies the process's standard input.\n\nIf Stdin is nil, the process reads from the null device (os.DevNull).\n\nIf Stdin is an *os.File, the process's standard input is connected\ndirectly to that file.\n\nOtherwise, during the execution of the command a separate\ngoroutine reads from Stdin and delivers that data to the command\nover a pipe. In this case, Wait does not complete until the goroutine\nstops copying, either because it has reached the end of Stdin\n(EOF or a read error), or because writing to the pipe returned an error,\nor because a nonzero WaitDelay was set and expired.\n\nRegardless of WaitDelay, Wait can block until a Read from\nStdin completes. If you need to use a blocking io.Reader,\nuse the StdinPipe method to get a pipe, copy from the Reader\nto the pipe, and arrange to close the Reader after Wait returns.\n"} Stdin























    ^{:line 235 :tag io/Writer :doc "Stdout and Stderr specify the process's standard output and error.\n\nIf either is nil, Run connects the corresponding file descriptor\nto the null device (os.DevNull).\n\nIf either is an *os.File, the corresponding output from the process\nis connected directly to that file.\n\nOtherwise, during the execution of the command a separate goroutine\nreads from the process over a pipe and delivers that data to the\ncorresponding Writer. In this case, Wait does not complete until the\ngoroutine reaches EOF or encounters an error or a nonzero WaitDelay\nexpires.\n\nRegardless of WaitDelay, Wait can block until a Write to\nStdout or Stderr completes. If you need to use a blocking io.Writer,\nuse the StdoutPipe or StderrPipe method to get a pipe,\ncopy from the pipe to the Writer, and arrange to close the\nWriter after Wait returns.\n\nIf Stdout and Stderr are the same writer, and have a type that can\nbe compared with ==, at most one goroutine at a time will call Write.\n"} Stdout
    ^{:line 236 :tag io/Writer} Stderr






    ^{:line 243 :tag (slice (* os/File)) :doc "ExtraFiles specifies additional open files to be inherited by the\nnew process. It does not include standard input, standard output, or\nstandard error. If non-nil, entry i becomes file descriptor 3+i.\n\nExtraFiles is not supported on Windows.\n"} ExtraFiles



    ^{:line 247 :tag (* syscall/SysProcAttr) :doc "SysProcAttr holds optional, operating system-specific attributes.\nRun passes it to os.StartProcess as the os.ProcAttr's Sys field.\n"} SysProcAttr


    ^{:line 250 :tag (* os/Process) :doc "Process is the underlying process, once started.\n"} Process




    ^{:line 255 :tag (* os/ProcessState) :doc "ProcessState contains information about an exited process.\nIf the process was started successfully, Wait or Run will\npopulate its ProcessState when the command completes.\n"} ProcessState


    ^{:line 258 :tag context/Context :doc "ctx is the context passed to CommandContext, if any.\n"} ctx

